<BaseLayout title="Professional Service & Outreach | Trevor Smith, Ph.D." description="Peer review, IEEE membership, robotics-team mentorship, K–12 competition volunteering, and public robotics outreach.">
  <section class="page-hero shell"><p class="eyebrow">Professional service & outreach</p><h1>Contributing to the field.<br>Opening doors to it.</h1><p>I support robotics at all levels through professional peer review, sustained undergraduate mentorship, and hands-on K-12 outreach.</p></section>
  <section class="service-professional"><div class="shell service-columns"><div><p class="eyebrow">Professional community</p><h2>Peer review and membership</h2><p>IEEE member since 2021 and reviewer for leading robotics journals and conferences.</p></div><div class="review-list"><div><strong>IEEE Robotics and Automation Letters</strong><span>11 reviews</span></div><div><strong>IEEE/RSJ International Conference on Intelligent Robots and Systems</strong><span>9 reviews</span></div><div><strong>International Journal of Social Robotics</strong><span>6 reviews</span></div><div><strong>IEEE International Conference on Robotics and Automation</strong><span>3 reviews</span></div></div></div></section>
  <section class="outreach-feature shell"><div><img src="/images/service/vex-team.jpg" alt="West Virginia University VEX-U robotics team"></div><div><p class="eyebrow">WVU Robotics Club · VEX-U</p><h2>A team that teaches beyond its own lab.</h2><p>As former Robotics Club president and graduate mentor, I led the club's growth from 6 to 30 active members, established a dedicated labspace, and built a VEX-U program that qualified for the World Championship annually from 2021 through 2025.</p><p>The team also volunteers at local VEX competitions. By judging, scorekeeping, coordinating matches, and discussing robot designs, we use competition to teach K–12 students about the engineering design process, teamwork, and robotics.</p></div></section>
  <section class="service-gallery"><img src="/images/service/vex-event.jpg" alt="Students and volunteers at a VEX robotics event"><div><p class="eyebrow">Competition volunteering · 2019–2025</p><strong>250+ students supported by robotics competitions</strong><p>Field operations, scoring, judging, match coordination, and informal STEM instruction.</p></div></section>
  <section class="lab-tours shell"><div><p class="eyebrow">Laboratory outreach</p><h2>Robotics should be encountered, not merely described.</h2></div><div class="lab-tours-media"><img src="/images/service/outreach.png" alt="Students participating in a hands-on robotics laboratory outreach visit"><p>Through regular K–12 lab visits and demonstrations, I introduce students to robots as systems they can question, touch, test, and imagine themselves building. These small-group visits—typically 10–20 students, roughly five times per year—make advanced robotics tangible and create room for genuine conversation.</p></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
